        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "bed95e00-d013-5928-8c98-cd39a2a73625",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Franklin Pierce, Lewis Cass, James Buchanan, Stephen A. Douglas, Andrew Jackson, Duncan McArthur\n- Events: 1852 Democratic National Convention, Treaty of Fort Meigs, Indian Removal Act, Treaty of Chicago\n- Organizations: United States Senate, United States government\n- Places: Michigan, Pennsylvania, Illinois\n- Topics: Native American treaties, Indian removal\n\nFacts:\n- On September 29, 1817, the Treaty of Fort Meigs was signed between the United States and chiefs and warriors of the Wyandot, Seneca, Delaware, Shawnee, Potawatomi, Ottawa, and Chippewa tribes.\n- Lewis Cass and Duncan McArthur represented the United States in the 1817 Treaty of Fort Meigs.\n- The Treaty of Fort Meigs of 1817 outlined voluntary Indian emigrations.\n- The Treaty of Chicago of 1821 also outlined voluntary Indian emigrations.\n- The Indian Removal Act made the voluntary emigrations in the Treaty of Fort Meigs and the Treaty of Chicago mandatory and militarily enforced.\n- Lewis Cass was born on October 9, 1782, and died on June 17, 1866.\n- Lewis Cass was an American military officer, politician, and statesman.\n- Lewis Cass represented Michigan in the United States Senate.\n- Lewis Cass served in the cabinets of Andrew Jackson and James Buchanan.\n- In 1848, Lewis Cass was the Democratic nominee for president.\n- In 1852, the Democratic National Convention nominated Franklin Pierce for president on the 49th ballot.\n- The 1852 Democratic National Convention passed over better known candidates Lewis Cass of Michigan, James Buchanan of Pennsylvania, and Stephen A. Douglas of Illinois in favor of Franklin Pierce.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "1871cad9-8a01-56dc-9750-12e96409c787",
        "text": "Page: 1852 Democratic National Convention. Sentence: The 1852 Democratic National Convention nominated the dark horse candidate Franklin Pierce for President on the 49th ballot, passing over better known candidates Lewis Cass of Michigan (the previous nominee in 1848), James Buchanan of Pennsylvania, and Stephen A. Douglas of Illinois.\nPage: Lewis Cass. Sentence: Lewis Cass (October 9, 1782June 17, 1866) was an American military officer, politician, and statesman.\nPage: Lewis Cass. Sentence: He represented Michigan in the United States Senate and served in the Cabinet of Andrew Jackson and James Buchanan.\nPage: Treaty of Fort Meigs. Sentence: The Treaty of Fort Meigs, also called the Treaty of the Foot of the Rapids, was signed September 29, 1817 between the chiefs and warriors of the Wyandot, Seneca, Delaware, Shawnee, Potawatomi, Ottawa and Chippewa, tribes of Native Americans and the United States of America, represented by Lewis Cass and Duncan McArthur.\nPage: Baw Beese. Sentence: The Indian Removal Act made the voluntary Indian emigrations outlined in the Treaty of Fort Meigs of 1817 and the Treaty of Chicago of 1821 mandatory and militarily enforced.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
